es/frame_3181.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_3182.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_3183.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_3184.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_3185.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_3186.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_3187.jpg'], 'response': 'Based on a detailed analysis of the 30 provided video frames, here is the assessment of the Nobel Peace Prize award presentation format:\n\n---\n\n**1) Award presentation method (in-person, video call, telephone, broadcast)**  \n→ **In-person.** All interactions occur physically on stage. The presenter and recipients are in the same room, standing face-to-face. No evidence of video call, telephone, or remote broadcast is present during the actual award handover.\n\n**2) Physical presence of award recipients on stage**  \n→ **Yes.** Recipients are physically present on stage, walking to the podium, receiving the award, shaking hands, and bowing. For example, Frame 5 shows a recipient walking toward the presenter, and Frame 8–11 show direct physical interaction.\n\n**3) Use of video screens or telecommunication equipment**  \n→ **Minimal and contextual.** Frame 1 shows a split-screen format, likely for broadcast or archival purposes, showing two different scenes simultaneously — one with presenters holding certificates and another with a recipient at a podium. However, this appears to be a production choice for viewers, not a method of award delivery. No telecommunication equipment (e.g., microphones for remote speakers, video feeds of absent recipients) is used to conduct the award ceremony itself.\n\n**4) Interaction between presenter and recipient**  \n→ **Direct, physical, and ceremonial.** The presenter shakes hands, hands over a physical diploma and medal, and exchanges bows or nods. This is visible in Frames 8–11, 17–22, and 25–27.\n\n**5) Method of handing over award (physical object vs virtual)**  \n→ **Physical object.** The award consists of a diploma and medal, both physically handed over. In Frame 9, the recipient receives a folded document (diploma) and a medal is visible around his neck in later frames (e.g., Frame 13).\n\n**6) Audience reaction to presentation format**  \n→ **Positive and formal.** The audience, dressed in formal attire, applauds after each award is presented (e.g., Frame 22, 27, 28). Their reaction is consistent with a traditional, in-person ceremony — no surprise or adjustment to virtual formats is observed.\n\n---\n\n**Verification of Answer ‘2’ (Adopted the method of broadcasting and connecting awards):**\n\nThe phrase “broadcasting and connecting awards” is ambiguous but appears to suggest that the awards were delivered via broadcast (i.e., remotely or virtually connected). **This is incorrect based on the evidence.**\n\n- While the event is *broadcast* (as most Nobel ceremonies are), the *presentation itself* is **in-person**.\n- The “connecting” aspect (if interpreted as connecting remotely via video/telecom) is **not** used for the actual award handover.\n- The split-screen in Frame 1 may reflect a broadcast edit or simultaneous coverage, but it does not indicate that the award was *delivered* remotely.\n\n**Conclusion: Answer ‘2’ is NOT correct.** The ceremony clearly follows an **in-person** format for award presentation, with physical handover of objects and direct interaction. The use of broadcasting is for audience dissemination, not for connecting absent recipients.\n\n---\n\n✅ Final Answer: **The Nobel Peace Prize award presentation in these frames was conducted in-person, with physical presence of recipients, direct interaction, and handover of physical awards. The format does not match “broadcasting and connecting awards” as described in answer ‘2’.**'}, {'batch_start': 60, 'batch_end': 64, 'image_paths': ['/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_3188.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_3189.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_3190.jpg', '/mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_3191.jpg'], 'response': 'Based on the visual evidence from the four frames provided, here is the analysis of the Nobel Peace Prize award presentation format:\n\n---\n\n**1) Award presentation method:**  \n✅ **In-person** — The frames show a formal ceremony taking place on a physical stage in a large auditorium. The presenter and recipient are both physically present on stage, interacting directly.\n\n**2) Physical presence of award recipients on stage:**  \n✅ **Yes** — The recipient is clearly standing on stage, receiving the award from the presenter. In Frame 1, the recipient (a woman) is seen accepting the award from a man in formal attire (likely the presenter or Nobel Committee representative).\n\n**3) Use of video screens or telecommunication equipment:**  \n❌ **No visible use** — There are no video screens, monitors, or telecommunication devices (e.g., microphones for remote connection, video call setups) indicating remote participation. The ceremony appears entirely live and in-person.\n\n**4) Interaction between presenter and recipient:**  \n✅ **Direct, face-to-face interaction** — In Frame 1, the presenter is handing the award to the recipient with a handshake or gesture of presentation. In Frames 2–3, the presenter bows slightly, indicating a formal, personal exchange.\n\n**5) Method of handing over award:**  \n✅ **Physical object** — The award (likely a medal and diploma) is handed over as a tangible item. The recipient is seen holding it in Frame 1.\n\n**6) Audience reaction to presentation format:**  \n✅ **Audience is physically present and applauding** — Frame 4 shows a large, live audience seated in the auditorium, clapping and observing the ceremony. There is no indication of virtual or remote audience participation.\n\n---\n\n**Verification of Answer ‘2’ (Adopted the method of broadcasting and connecting awards):**\n\n❌ **Incorrect.**  \nThe phrase “broadcasting and connecting awards” implies a remote or mediated format — such as live broadcast with virtual connections, video calls, or teleconferencing — which is **not** what is depicted.\n\nThe frames clearly show:\n- A **live, in-person ceremony**.\n- **No remote participants**.\n- **No video links or telecommunication equipment** in use.\n- **Direct physical handover** of the award.\n\nThus, the format is **not** “broadcasting and connecting awards” — it is a **traditional in-person award ceremony**.\n\n---\n\n✅ **Final Conclusion:**  \nAnswer ‘2’ is **incorrect**. The Nobel Peace Prize in these frames is presented **in-person**, not via broadcasting or remote connection methods. The correct format is **physical, live, and face-to-face**.'}], 'formatted_responses': ['\n=== Batch 0-30 (30 frames) ===\nFrames: /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0726.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0727.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0728.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0729.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0730.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0731.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0732.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0733.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0734.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0735.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0736.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0750.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/8NHmfpkxTSw/frames/frame_0751.jpg, /mnt/ssd/d